A `for` over a const list declares one window per item: title and href come
from the item, so a two-element `items` is one.html and two.html. The loop's
window id is itself what the index window iterates -- `for var p = page` walks
the windows the first loop declared, and each `html.a` takes that page's
folded href and title.

Both loops run at build time. The golden is three static pages whose links are
literal, with no client JS behind them.

The body reads `page.title` beside `it.title`, and the two fold by different
routes. `it.title` is the loop variable the unroll bound. `page.title` is a
read off the window's own id, which folds to the window's `title` *expression*
-- itself `it.title`, written one line up and not yet substituted. Returned as
written it stayed `it.title` in a body where the unroll had already passed, and
the page rendered an empty span; it is folded again against the context the
read sits in, which is where `it` is bound.

-- site.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:platform/html"

output { none { html } }

struct Item {
    slug string = ""
    title string = ""
}

const items list<Item> = [
    {slug="one", title="One"},
    {slug="two", title="Two"},
]

window #index(title="Index", href="/index.html") {
    for var p = page {
        html.a(href=p.href, innerText=p.title)
    }
}

for var it = items {
    window #page(title=it.title, href="/" + it.slug + ".html") {
        text(value=it.title)
        text(value=page.title)
    }
}
-- out/none/html/index.html --
